@startuml userid-via-whoami-headers
title Kubernetes + oauth2-proxy + Okta\nAngular SPA gets the userId from X-Auth-Request-* headers on a static whoami.txt

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center

actor User
participant "Browser\n(Angular SPA)" as B
box "Kubernetes cluster" #F7F7F7
    participant "Ingress\n(routes app.example.com\nto oauth2-proxy Service)" as I
    participant "oauth2-proxy\nDeployment/Service\n(--set-xauthrequest=true)" as P
    participant "Angular assets\nnginx Deployment/Service\n(incl. /assets/whoami.txt)" as S
end box
participant "Okta\n(OIDC provider)" as O

== Unauthenticated request: standard OIDC authorization-code sign-in ==

User -> B: Open https://app.example.com
B -> I: GET / (no session cookie)
I -> P: Forward
P --> B: 302 Location: https://{okta-domain}/oauth2/default/v1/authorize?...
B -> O: GET /authorize, user signs in\n(credentials, MFA, ...)
O --> B: 302 Location: https://app.example.com/oauth2/callback?code=...&state=...
B -> I: GET /oauth2/callback?code=...
I -> P: Forward
P -> O: POST /token (exchange authorization code)
O --> P: ID token (sub, email, preferred_username claims)
P --> B: Set-Cookie: _oauth2_proxy=<encrypted session>\n(HttpOnly, Secure) + 302 to original URL
note over B, P
  The browser now holds only the encrypted session cookie.
  No token is ever stored in - or visible to - JavaScript.
end note

== Angular app loads ==

B -> I: GET / \nCookie: _oauth2_proxy=...
I -> P: Forward
P -> P: Decrypt/validate session cookie
P -> S: GET /index.html
S --> P: 200 index.html + JS bundles
P --> B: 200 - Angular app boots

== SPA startup: fetch userId via whoami.txt ==

B -> B: IdentityService: HttpClient GET\n/assets/whoami.txt (observe: 'response')
B -> I: GET /assets/whoami.txt\nCookie: _oauth2_proxy=...
I -> P: Forward
P -> P: Decrypt cookie, load session claims\n(cached from the Okta ID token)
P -> S: GET /assets/whoami.txt
S --> P: 200 OK (empty/one-line body)
P --> B: 200 OK\nX-Auth-Request-User: <sub or email>\nX-Auth-Request-Email: <email>\nX-Auth-Request-Preferred-Username: <preferred_username>
note right of B
  Same-origin request, so JavaScript
  may read all response headers -
  no CORS configuration needed.
end note
B -> B: userId = response.headers\n.get('X-Auth-Request-User')

note over B, S
  All standard building blocks: a stock Kubernetes Ingress, a stock oauth2-proxy
  deployment in reverse-proxy (upstream) mode with --set-xauthrequest=true, and
  Okta's standard authorization-code flow. API calls follow the same path: the
  proxy swaps the cookie for "Authorization: Bearer <id-token>" toward the
  .NET API Service (--pass-authorization-header=true).

  Caching caveat: the headers come from oauth2-proxy, not the file. Serve
  whoami.txt with Cache-Control: no-store (or cache-bust the URL) so the
  request always reaches the proxy.
end note

@enduml
